455-There are many factors affecting the extent to which children participate other than the design of a programme. The ability
456-of a child to participate, for example, varies greatly with his development: a preschool child may be only capable of car-
457-rying materials to a playground building site, whereas an adolescent might be able to oversee the entire building opera-
458-tion. This question, together with some of the subtle cultural issues affecting children’s participation, will be considered
459:in a subsequent section. Also, it is not necessary that children always operate on the highest possible rungs of the ladder.
460-Different children at different times might prefer to perform with varying degrees of involvement or responsibility. The
461-important principle again is one of choice: programmes should be designed which maximize the opportunity for any child
462:to choose to participate at the highest level of his ability.
